The dma-buf UAPI lives under <drm/>, not under a kernel-specific directory.

USE_GBM=ON compiles the GBM graphics path for the first time in this port,
and its CPU-access bracket includes the dma-buf UAPI at the path the
reference kernel installs it to:

    MemoryMappedGPUBuffer.cpp:39:10: fatal error: linux/dma-buf.h:
      No such file or directory

Everything it needs from that header is already present and correct.  This
libc installs the same UAPI -- struct dma_buf_sync, the DMA_BUF_SYNC_* flags
and DMA_BUF_IOCTL_SYNC, byte for byte from the same source -- as
<drm/dma-buf.h>, beside drm.h, drm_mode.h, drm_fourcc.h and sync_file.h, the
other four headers this file's neighbours already include that way.  The
ioctl behind it is implemented too: kernel/dev/gpu/drm/drm_gem.c dispatches
_IOC_TYPE(req) == DMA_BUF_BASE and handles request 0 by waiting for the
device before the CPU touches the mapping.

So this is a spelling difference and nothing more, and the arm is written to
say only that: on this system the header is <drm/dma-buf.h>, everywhere else
it is where it always was.

Not fixed by adding the other directory to the sysroot: that would put a
second copy of one UAPI on the include path under a name that promises a
kernel this is not, and the next port to reach for something else under it
would find a directory that exists and a header that does not.

--- a/Source/WebCore/platform/graphics/gbm/MemoryMappedGPUBuffer.cpp
+++ b/Source/WebCore/platform/graphics/gbm/MemoryMappedGPUBuffer.cpp
@@ -36,7 +36,11 @@
 #include "VivanteSuperTiledTextureInlines.h"
 #include <epoxy/egl.h>
 #include <fcntl.h>
+#if defined(__LIKEOS__)
+#include <drm/dma-buf.h>
+#else
 #include <linux/dma-buf.h>
+#endif
 #include <sys/ioctl.h>
 #include <sys/mman.h>
 #include <wtf/SafeStrerror.h>
